     1	# Chapter 23 — The Registry Line
     2	
     3	The east road out of Ostrand's province ran four days, and the country thinned as it went, and the infrastructure thinned with it — and Cael catalogued the thinning, because cataloguing was how he loved things, and because the thinning was the last argument the road had to make and it made it in geography.
     4	
     5	He kept the count in the observation notebook, under a heading he ruled on the first morning and did not explain to anybody: what the road still had.
     6	
     7	The first day it had everything. Milestones with the province's mark cut in them at the half-league. A toll at the second bridge with a bored man in it and a printed schedule of rates nailed to the post. Four inns, of which three were chartered. Carters going both ways at a rate of one every quarter-hour, and among them, twice, the district post. Two grey coats, at the toll and at the third crossroads, standing where grey coats stand. And a boy at a hamlet's edge who asked Brom, without preamble, what tier he was, because a big man on a cart is a question a boy in a province asks, and Brom said "Copper" and the boy said "oh" and lost interest entirely, which made Lira laugh for a mile.
     8	
     9	The second day it had less. Milestones at the league instead of the half. One inn. No toll. Carters at perhaps one an hour, and the composition of them changed: fewer goods, more people, and the people carrying their own goods. The prices went up, which Karis noted with professional interest and entered — a loaf at the second day's inn cost a third more than a loaf at the first day's, in a country with more grain in it, because the cost of a thing on a road is not the cost of making it but the cost of the last man who was willing to bring it.
    10	
    11	And on the second afternoon, the last chartered waystation.
    12	
    13	It was a coaching inn of the ordinary kind, stone below and timber above, a yard, a trough, six stalls of which four were empty, and the registry's own furniture still on its wall by the door: the posting-board, with the small sigil at its head and everything the registry required a chartered house to display, current, kept, and completely useless.
    14	
    15	Cael read the board the way he read all boards, standing in the yard with the horses being watered behind him.
    16	
    17	*By charter of the registry, this house being chartered: TIER VERIFICATION available at the district station, Ostrand, four days west. No station lies east of the river crossing, and the station at the ford is unstaffed. TRAVEL ENDORSEMENTS may be lodged at this house for forward transmission; allow twenty days. ENROLLMENT INQUIRIES by the same. The chartered rates of this house are posted within. Complaints of a chartered house lie to the district seat.*
    18	
    19	Twenty days for a travel endorsement to be transmitted from a house four days from the station that would receive it. He worked the arithmetic out of habit and got the answer the road had been giving him all afternoon, which was that everything on the board was true and nothing on it was usable, and that the registry's presence out here was a thing kept in repair rather than a thing in use — the way a household keeps a clock in a room nobody sits in.
    20	
    21	The keeper was a woman of sixty who had run the house for thirty years and had views. She told them, unasked, what the road did ahead: the hamlet on the third morning; the provincial marker after it; the ford on the fourth afternoon and the old station on the far bank; and nothing after that except road. She told them that the post-rider came out from the district twice a week, ran his eastern leg to the ford, watered, and turned; that anything going west was handed to him at the ford or at her counter and nowhere else; and that in thirty years she had watched about two dozen parties go east and had seen five of them come back, and that she said so to everybody and it stopped nobody, and she had stopped expecting it to.
    22	
    23	"Most go east because something's behind them," she said, filling Brom's flask. "Yours is a strange one. You've boxes. Nobody with something behind them stops to rope boxes."
    24	
    25	"We're not being chased," Karis said. "We resigned."
    26	
    27	The keeper considered that for a moment and evidently found it the most interesting sentence anybody had said in her yard that season. "Then you'll do better than most," she said, and went in.
    28	
    29	Cael made two entries about the house before they left it. The first was that it took the registry's charter money and displayed the registry's board and had done for thirty years, and that in the yard the woman priced her rooms by what a traveler looked like he could pay, which is not a chartered rate, and that nobody within four days could have told her otherwise. The second was that when Lira asked her, at the door, what she would do if the district ever stopped sending the charter money, she said, "Same as now, only with a nail-hole where the board was," and laughed, and that the laugh was the most accurate assessment of the Compact's reach anyone had offered him all year, and had come from a woman who filled flasks.
    30	
    31	---
    32	
    33	The third morning gave up the last posted tier-verification notice.
    34	
    35	It was nailed to a boundary marker at a hamlet's edge, weathered soft, a year old at least, the standard notice in the standard type — *a registry station lies within four days' travel west; no station lies east; practitioners are reminded that tier certification is verifiable only at station* — and somebody had, at some point, used the bottom third of it to work out a sum in charcoal about hay.
    36	
    37	Past the hamlet the markers stopped carrying registry sigils. They went on carrying distances for a while, and then stopped carrying those too, and after that they were simply stones by a road that somebody long ago had troubled to stand upright, and Cael entered the transition at the exact stone where it happened, because the exact stone was the kind of thing his notebook existed for.
    38	
    39	The third afternoon gave up the last watcher.
    40	
    41	He was at the provincial boundary marker: one man, in a grey coat, on a good horse, holding his position at the marker the way a man holds a position at the end of a leash — because that was what he was; the registry's reach ended where the stations ended, and a watcher four days east of the last staffed station was a watcher watching the edge of his own jurisdiction, with orders that ran to the marker and no further, and nothing east of him but a job description that stopped.
    42	
    43	He had them in his glass for a long while before the cart drew level. Cael, who had spent four years being looked at through glasses, ran the man's procedure and found it correct and unhurried: the glass, the small book, the entry, the glass again. Five travelers, one cart, one direction. Five names if he had them, which he did, because the resignations had been filed at a counter three days ago and the counter's ledger went where such ledgers went.
    44	
    45	And as the cart drew level, he did the one thing his procedure did not require.
    46	
    47	He put the glass down, and inclined his head to Brom. Not to the cart. Not to the party. To the man on the driving bench with the continental Copper title on a plaque in a kit at his feet, from a career surveillance officer at the end of his leash, professional to professional, in a country with nobody in it to see him do it.
    48	
    49	Brom returned it with the same economy, and neither man said anything, and the watcher turned his horse back west, because there was nowhere further east for the leash to reach and both of them knew it.
    50	
    51	They went half a mile before anybody spoke.
    52	
    53	"That was a nod," Lira said.
    54	
    55	"That was a nod," Brom agreed.
    56	
    57	"He's been logging us for a year and a half."
    58	
    59	"He has." Brom shook the reins. "He also watched me win a continental final in the rain and had to write down what time I left the ground. Man's entitled to a nod."
    60	
    61	Seln, from the cart's tail, delivered the professional footnote without opening his eyes. "He'll have entered the courtesy. They enter everything." A pause. "He'll have entered it as *no incident*, which is the correct entry, and which is also — from that grade, in that coat, at that marker — the kindest sentence available to him. Take it as meant."
    62	
    63	---
    64	
    65	The fourth afternoon gave up the last Arbiter station.
    66	
    67	It stood at the river crossing: a small, old, well-kept post on the near bank, one room and a slate roof and a stone apron in front of it worn hollow by generations of feet that had stood in exactly one place. The last of the network. The practical eastern terminus of the whole apparatus that had measured every person on the continent for four hundred years, and it was the size of a granary.
    68	
    69	Cael went over it the way he went over instruments.
    70	
    71	The door was not locked and had not been locked in years; there was no lock. The room held a bench, a swept floor, a shelf with nothing on it, and the frame. The frame was the standard frame — the same architecture as the one at Denvash, the same as the district station at Ostrand, four uprights and a lintel of the dressed grey stone the network had always used, with the sigil set into the lintel's underside in its housing. The stone was clean. The apron outside had been swept, this season, by somebody. The roof's slates had been repaired at the north corner with slates that did not match, which meant a person had climbed onto the roof of an unstaffed station at the edge of the administered world and mended it with what was to hand, and had done it well.
    72	
    73	The sigil was dark in its housing. No evaluand, no evaluator; a frame kept in repair by a keeper never seen, against a traffic that had dwindled to nothing.
    74	
    75	Karis found the ledger on the shelf's underside, because Karis found ledgers. It was a station book of the ordinary kind, half full, and the entries in it ran back eleven years and stopped four years ago: Kindling evaluations, two and three a year, children brought down out of the hills by parents who had walked them two and three days to be measured, each entry a date, a name, and a classification in a station officer's hand. The last dozen were in a different hand from the first hundred. The last one of all had a date and a name and, in the classification column, nothing — the line left open, as though the officer had intended to come back to it.
    76	
    77	"Somebody stopped coming," Karis said. She turned the leaf and found no more. "The station wasn't closed. Nobody closed it. The officer stopped being sent, and the book stayed on the shelf, and the roof got mended by whoever mends the roof, and the frame sat here dark for four years while children in those hills went unmeasured." She put the book back exactly where it had been, which was her one superstition. "There are people out there of eleven and twelve years old who have never been classified as anything. I would like it entered that I find that the most hopeful fact I have encountered in four years of studying this apparatus, and that I am aware of what it says about me."
    78	
    79	The five of them passed it one at a time, at the ford, because the road went past the apron and the apron was the only good footing to the water, and each of them had a different account to settle with the instrument.
    80	
    81	Lira, who had been measured wrong by one of these at fourteen, and had spent six years proving the measurement wrong, crossed it without a glance, which was its own kind of glance.
    82	
    83	Brom, who had been priced by one — Copper, forever, the number his family's ledger demanded and his architecture exceeded — crossed it and touched the housing once, briefly, in passing, the way a man touches a doorframe on his way out of a house he is done with.
    84	
    85	Karis, who had studied these instruments until she found the maintenance records of their oldest stratum, crossed it and looked at it the longest, and said nothing, because she had said it all in an annex, and what was left was the look a researcher gives the primary source when she has finally understood it and is walking away from it anyway.
    86	
    87	Seln, who had serviced the machine these frames fed, crossed it and did not look at it at all, which from Seln was the loudest crossing of the three that had gone before.
    88	
    89	And Cael, last, stopped.
    90	
    91	The others took the cart down to the water. He stood on the swept apron in front of a dark frame at the end of the world's paperwork, alone, for about a minute.
    92	
    93	He had thought, on the road, that he might have something prepared, and had caught himself drafting it on the third evening and had stopped, because a speech to a machine is a speech to whoever you imagine is listening, and there was nobody. He had learned, across four years, the difference between a gesture aimed at a witness and a thing said to a thing.
    94	
    95	"You couldn't read me," he told it, conversationally, the way you tell a fact to an old machine. "Four years I thought that was the system failing. Turns out it was the most honest thing the system ever did — the one time the layer under you couldn't finish its job, because there was no bin beneath me to file the lie in." He looked at the dark frame a moment longer. "You're the only part of the whole apparatus that never lied about me. You just went quiet. Everything above you did the lying." He shouldered the satchel. "Thanks for the eleven seconds. They were the truest measurement I ever got."

exec
/bin/zsh -lc "nl -ba books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-23.md | sed -n '96,210p'" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
