    96	
    97	He walked on across the ford.
    98	
    99	The water came to the axle. On the far bank the road went up through gravel and out of the trees, and behind them the little station stood on its apron with its swept floor and its mended roof and its dark stone, going on being kept, by somebody, for nobody, at the edge of everything.
   100	
   101	---
   102	
   103	The Registry Line passed under the cart's wheels at mid-afternoon, unmarked.
   104	
   105	There was no marker, because the thing it bounded had no interest in being bounded. It was not a border; nobody had drawn it; no instrument on the continent recorded where it fell. It was simply the practical edge of the Arbiter network — the meter of road beyond which no station operated, no tier was verified, no enrollment existed, and no asset-restriction could execute, because there was no registry east of it to execute in.
   106	
   107	The cart's wheels crossed it. The country did not change. The road ran on into the same thinning hills it had run through all afternoon, past the same gorse, under the same sky, and the five of them crossed the most consequential line any of them would ever cross without a single one of them knowing, in the moment, which meter of road it had been.
   108	
   109	Cael tried, afterward, to fix it. He worked backward from the ford and the pace and the light, and got it to within perhaps two hundred yards, and then stopped, and wrote down that he had stopped and why: that the whole point of the thing was that it was not a place, and that a man who insisted on marking it would only have been building one more station.
   110	
   111	Lira was the one who said it, an hour later, realizing — reining the lead horse a moment, looking back west and then east and then at the others, with the specific stillness of a person checking a fact she cannot quite believe is a fact.
   112	
   113	"Nobody's watching."
   114	
   115	Four years of rotations, watchers, windows, files, escorts, seals, glasses logging them through — and an hour of road, past a line no one had marked, where the sentence was simply true. No grey coat at any distance. No glass. No paper coming up any road under any seal. The country held them the way country holds anything, indifferently, enormously, and did not report them to anyone, because there was nobody east of the Line for the country to report to.
   116	
   117	"Nobody's watching," Cael agreed, and charted the absence the way he had charted the presence for four years, and found the chart had no entries in it, and left the page blank, because a blank page was the honest record of the thing.
   118	
   119	What he did not write, and turned over for the rest of the afternoon, was that he had been watched for so long that he had stopped being able to tell which of his habits were his. The counting of exits. The reading of a room's grades before its faces. The four-year practice of never letting a true sentence out of his mouth in a corridor. He had built an entire competence out of being observed, had been praised for it, had beaten a hearing and a tournament and a term of proceedings with it — and somewhere on this stretch of road the thing it was built against had simply stopped, and the competence was still running, with nothing to do, like a mill wheel turning in a dry race.
   120	
   121	He decided, provisionally, to keep it. Karis was right that assuming the reach ended with the stations was bad method. But he entered the other half honestly, because the log did not take summaries: *the habits stay. The reason for them just went away, and I noticed how much of me was the reason.*
   122	
   123	The others took it in their own currencies, which he collected, because he collected.
   124	
   125	Karis said it was, technically, incorrect — that there was no reason to assume the faceless layer's reach ended with its stations, and that they should behave for a year as though it did not — and then said, in a different voice entirely, "And I am aware that I have just declined to enjoy the first hour of this in my life, and I would like that noted as a defect of character rather than of method."
   126	
   127	Brom said nothing at all for two miles and then said, "Nobody's counting the horse either," which was the most cheerful sentence anybody had heard out of him since Norhold.
   128	
   129	And Seln said, "It ends where the stations end. That is a fact about them and not about us," and then, after a while, in the same flat administrative register, "It took me eleven years to be certain of that. You have it on your first afternoon. Do not waste it by being careless with it," which was, from that man, an entire benediction.
   130	
   131	---
   132	
   133	They made the fourth night's camp two hours past the Line, in a fold of ground with the road above it, and it was the first night any of them had ever slept outside a jurisdiction.
   134	
   135	Nobody said that out loud. What happened instead was that the ordinary business of a camp got done, and every one of them was audibly and visibly not saying it, and the not-saying gave the evening a strange, light, unmoored quality that Cael tried three times to name in the notebook and gave up on. Brom cooked. Lira wanted to know what the watch arrangement was and Seln said there was no watch arrangement out here that was worth losing sleep over yet and would be within a week, and set one anyway, and took the worst of it, and denied that he had. Karis worked by firelight with the boxes open, which she had not been able to do in a coach and had evidently been suffering about for four days.
   136	
   137	And at some point in it Lira said, to the fire, "I keep waiting to be nervous," and nobody answered her, and about a minute later Brom said, "Give it a week," and that was the whole conversation and it was, Cael thought, entirely sufficient.
   138	
   139	The Daeva deferral was paid at the fourth night's camp, in the last of the light, on a knee, with the fire behind him.
   140	
   141	Her letter had come in the term's second month, direct and without comfort, and had ended with an instruction rather than a wish, and he had carried it in the satchel through a ruling and a resignation and four days of road without answering it, because the honest answer had not existed yet and he did not send unfinished ones.
   142	
   143	Now it existed. He wrote it short, in his own register, and did not draft it twice:
   144	
   145	*They ruled. We left — resigned, before the date, in good standing, four files closed complete and one man who filed nothing. You said keep myself unclassified. Done, permanently, on my own paper this time: I'm past the Registry Line, where the word has no meaning, which turns out to be the only place the word is safe.*
   146	
   147	*The rematch stands. Not postponed — relocated. It happens on a floor nobody owns, and I think you'd prefer that anyway; a Gold-tier fact and an unclassified one, fighting where no seal can score either of us, is the only honest version of the bout, and we both knew it at Norhold. Find me when you're ready to fight something no ladder built. I'll be east.*
   148	
   149	*— C.*
   150	
   151	He sealed it with nothing, because he had no seal any more and had not had one that meant anything at any point in his life, and addressed it to her academy's hall in the plain hand a stranger uses.
   152	
   153	In the morning it went back west with the waystation's post-rider, met at the ford at the eastern end of his run — a coaching house's man in a leather coat, watering his horse at the last Arbiter station's apron, who took the letter, glanced at the direction, glanced at it again, and said, "That's the champion," and then, after a moment's thought, decided against asking anything, and put it in the pouch, and rode west. He had no idea he was carrying the youngest Gold-tier in history's correspondence out of the administered world and into it, and Cael watched him go for a while, because that letter was the last piece of mail the registry's world would ever carry for him and it seemed worth watching leave.
   154	
   155	The log banked the thread beside its lineage:
   156	
