+# Movement 009 source-distance read
+
+## Verdict
+
+**Pass: 4 genuine tracked passages.** Chapters 56, 58 and 59 have none. Chapter 55 has one,
+Chapter 57 has one, and Chapter 60 has two. No scene contains more than one genuine tracked
+passage, so the movement does not read as a source-chapter paraphrase after the protected and
+packet material is removed.
+
+Counting unit: one continuous manuscript passage following one source sentence, or one tightly
+unified source sentence-cluster, counts as one passage. When a source sentence or compact cluster
+is distributed across several manuscript sentences, it is one **split** passage. Scene numbers
+follow the manuscript's `---` divisions.
+
+I excluded all Tier A/B and packet language, including Gault's note, the personnel query, the
+waystation board, the Arbiter-frame speech, the Daeva and closing letters, the final inventory,
+the closing exchange and the last line. I also excluded the packet's unusually detailed required
+sequence: the four leaves and forfeiture heads; the fifteen-minute class and corridor window;
+the three box trips, gate farewells and watchers; the empty-desk evidence, apparatus review and
+Seln road window; the machine's week and crossroads camp; the thinning-road catalogue, watcher,
+station, Line, post-rider and ridge; and the final camp's watch, load, archive, route, money,
+inventory, letters and faceless window wherever the manuscript supplies a new telling.
+
+| Manuscript chapter | Scene counts (in manuscript order) | Chapter total |
+|---|---|---:|
+| 55 | S1 counter/Lira and Brom: 0; S2 Cael/margin: 0; S3 Karis forfeiture: 0; S4 Bracken/Gault/assistant: 1; S5 Cael's room: 0 | **1** |
+| 56 | S1 last class: 0; S2 Lira window: 0; S3 Seln/Brom rooms: 0; S4 three boxes: 0; S5 gate: 0; S6 bluff road/log: 0 | **0** |
+| 57 | S1 empty desk: 1; S2 apparatus: 0; S3 stove/departure: 0; S4 east road/look back: 0; S5 crossroads/fire: 0 | **1** |
+| 58 | S1 machine's week: 0; S2 Vastin: 0; S3 east road: 0; S4 first ford/watcher: 0; S5 crossroads reunion: 0; S6 second ford: 0 | **0** |
+| 59 | S1 thinning road: 0; S2 last waystation: 0; S3 watcher/ferry: 0; S4 last station/crossings: 0; S5 unmarked Line: 0; S6 first night/letter: 0; S7 ridge: 0; S8 —: 0 | **0** |
+| 60 | S1 scree/shelf/camp: 1; S2 watch rota: 0; S3 cart/archive/money: 1; S4 inventory/anomaly: 0; S5 poles/letters: 0; S6 faceless file: 0; S7 closing exchange: 0 | **2** |
+| **Movement** |  | **4** |
+
+Chapter 59 has seven `---`-delimited scenes, not eight; the probe's sentence segmenter reports
+eight statistical scenes because it splits one prose run internally. The verdict uses the
+manuscript divisions, as above.
+
+The requested probe returned **3% skeleton / 15% close** overall across 1,553 sentences. Its
+hottest scenes were Chapter 59 scene 5 (**17% skeleton / 29% close**) and Chapter 60 scene 4
+(**11% / 33%**). Those rates are protection-driven: the first contains the protected Arbiter-frame
+speech and the second the protected closing inventory. The displayed exact pairs in Chapters 55,
+57, 59 and 60 are likewise chiefly Gault's note, the personnel query, the waystation board, the
+letters, inventory and closing material. The four findings below come from the side-by-side read,
+not from treating probe heat as a finding.
+
+## Tracked passages
+
+| chapter:line | Manuscript opening words | Source sentence it follows (file:line plus opening words) | Kind |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 55:175 | I believed then that if the house kept its record true… | `chapter-21.md:53` — “The Halcenvane enrollment defense…was the best-documented matter…” | varied |
+| 57:13 | The table was clear. It was not the clearness of a man gone for a moment… | `chapter-22.md:9` — “The desk was squared past its ordinary square. Not tidy — *finished*…” | varied |
+| 60:3 | The track went down the far side of the ridge by switchbacks, over scree… | `chapter-24.md:3–7` — “The first true edge-territories camp was a switchback shelf…” | split |
+| 60:157 | This is the first archive I have ever kept that nobody can close… | `chapter-24.md:21` — “It is also the first archive I have ever kept that nobody can close.” | near-verbatim |
+
+The Chapter 55 passage preserves the source speech's distinctive reasoning order: the record was
+true; Bracken believed truth would be enough; he has learned that it was not; he will keep true
+records anyway. The packet requires only that Bracken go off procedure, not this four-step
+reckoning.
+
+The Chapter 57 passage preserves the source's inference from arrangement rather than merely the
+required empty-desk facts: the surface is beyond ordinarily tidy, the writing tools are finished
+and aligned, the chair is pushed fully in, and the observer understands that the man has left
+rather than stepped away.
+
+The Chapter 60 opening retains source geography the packet explicitly leaves to the author: a
+descent over scree in switchbacks, the widening at a numbered turn, a narrow level shelf with rock
+at its back and a drop at its lip, then nearby water. The details are changed and expanded, but
+the source cluster's beats remain in the same sequence.
+
+The Chapter 60 archive sentence changes only its demonstrative and reporting frame. “Brom's load
+rule” and the existence of Karis's field archive are packet/BOOK_MAP requirements, but this exact
+conclusion about an archive “nobody can close” is not protected and remains near-verbatim.
+
+## Dense-scene re-entry suggestions
+
+None. No scene contains a cluster of genuine tracked passages. Chapter 60 scene 1 has one
+extended geography carryover, but not repeated source tracking through the rest of the scene; it
+is a focused rewrite candidate rather than a scene that needs a new entry point or reordered beat
+structure. The other three findings are line- or paragraph-level repairs.

